Understanding how treatment works helps you see the available options and make a decision with greater awareness. In upper limb surgery there are several techniques, each with specific indications. The choice depends on the type of lesion, tissue quality, patient characteristics and the functional outcome we aim to achieve.

These are the four main approaches I use in clinical practice.

# Arthroscopy

> Small portals, magnified vision and targeted treatment.

Arthroscopy is a minimally invasive surgical technique that allows operating inside the joints without exposing them through an extended incision.

It is performed through two or three small skin portals, just a few millimetres wide. A high-definition camera is introduced through one portal, projecting magnified images of the joint onto a monitor; surgical instruments are inserted through the others to carry out the procedure.

I use it primarily for rotator cuff repairs, shoulder instability treatment, glenoid labral tears, calcific tendinitis and selected forms of joint stiffness.

The main advantage is the ability to treat deep structures while better preserving the surrounding tissues. This can support a smoother post-operative recovery, reduce surgical trauma and allow progressive rehabilitation tailored to the specific procedure performed.

# Open surgery

> When direct access is needed to reconstruct, repair or replace.

Not all conditions can be treated effectively with arthroscopy. In some cases it is necessary to reach the anatomical structures directly through a wider incision, allowing work under direct vision.

Open surgery is indicated, for instance, for complex fractures, bone graft reconstructions, tendon or muscle transfers and several joint replacement procedures.

The surgical approach is chosen according to the structure to be treated and the reconstruction required. It is not a more invasive technique to be avoided at all costs, but the most appropriate tool when it delivers a safer, more reliable outcome.

# Shoulder replacement

> Anatomic or reverse: two different solutions, chosen according to the condition of the shoulder.

When the joint is severely damaged by arthritis, fracture or other conditions, replacing the joint surfaces with a prosthesis may be necessary.

There are two main types of shoulder prosthesis.

An **anatomic prosthesis** reproduces the natural shape of the joint. It is indicated primarily when the rotator cuff is intact and functioning, because those tendons provide shoulder movement and stability.

A **reverse prosthesis**, by changing the geometry of the joint, allows the deltoid muscle to take on a greater role in moving the arm and is particularly indicated when the rotator cuff is no longer functional, torn and irreparable, or in selected complex fractures in older adults.

Pre-operative planning is essential. I use three-dimensional CT reconstructions to study the patient's anatomy, assess bone quality and define implant sizes and positioning in advance. When indicated, I also use patient-specific surgical guides or computer-assisted intraoperative navigation systems.

The goal is to make the implant as precise and reproducible as possible, tailored to the characteristics of each individual shoulder.

# Interventional ultrasound

> Targeted treatments, guided in real time by ultrasound.

Not all conditions require surgery. Many tendon and joint problems can be treated in an outpatient clinic through minimally invasive procedures, without a scalpel.

Ultrasound provides real-time visualisation of tendons, muscles, bursae and joints, guiding instruments to the exact point to be treated.

This allows me to perform ultrasound-guided injections, percutaneous lavage of shoulder calcifications (*barbotage*) and other targeted interventional procedures.

The advantage is precise treatment of the affected structure, with continuous control of needle and instrument placement. These procedures are generally well tolerated and can avoid surgery whenever outpatient treatment is appropriately indicated.

# Before and after

{% affiancati %}
{% colonna titolo="Before" %}
Pre-operative visit with the anaesthetist, blood tests, ECG if needed. I explain what will happen, what to bring, what to expect.
{% /colonna %}
{% colonna titolo="After" %}
A sling for a period that depends on the procedure. A written exercise programme. Scheduled follow-ups. Your physiotherapist receives precise instructions.
